WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.530
Settle somewhere safe enough to pause.

2
00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.420
Sit, lie down, or stand with support.

3
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.970
Keep your eyes open if that helps you stay oriented.

4
00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:18.360
This practice does not ask you to breathe deeply or make stress disappear.

5
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:24.720
You can change position, return to ordinary breathing, or stop whenever you choose.

6
00:00:34.778 --> 00:00:38.328
Begin with contact rather than breath.

7
00:00:40.028 --> 00:00:45.728
Feel the floor under the feet, the chair beneath you, or another surface holding some of your weight.

8
00:00:47.428 --> 00:00:50.938
Notice one place where support is already present without effort from you.

9
00:01:06.026 --> 00:01:10.266
Look around and let your eyes find three ordinary shapes or colors.

10
00:01:11.966 --> 00:01:14.356
Nothing needs to be beautiful or meaningful.

11
00:01:16.056 --> 00:01:20.996
Let the room be part of the practice, so attention does not have to stay entirely inside the body.

12
00:01:36.084 --> 00:01:38.524
Now notice that breathing is happening.

13
00:01:40.224 --> 00:01:41.134
Do not improve it.

14
00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:47.434
The breath may be quick, shallow, uneven, held, or hard to locate.

15
00:01:49.134 --> 00:01:51.334
Begin with the breath you actually have.

16
00:02:09.439 --> 00:02:18.299
Choose one place where breathing is easiest to sense: air at the nostrils, movement in the chest, the sides of the ribs, or the abdomen meeting clothing.

17
00:02:19.999 --> 00:02:25.649
If none of these feels clear, feel a hand resting on the body and notice the movement through the hand.

18
00:02:45.766 --> 00:02:48.406
Stay alongside one inhale from beginning to end.

19
00:02:50.106 --> 00:02:51.406
Then one exhale.

20
00:02:53.106 --> 00:02:55.936
There is no need to count or lengthen either one.

21
00:02:57.636 --> 00:03:00.006
Let the next breath arrive in its own time.

22
00:03:25.152 --> 00:03:27.432
Stress can make the breath feel like another task.

23
00:03:29.132 --> 00:03:32.622
If you notice yourself trying hard, widen attention.

24
00:03:34.322 --> 00:03:38.852
Include the surface beneath you, sounds in the room, and the space around the breath.

25
00:03:40.552 --> 00:03:43.692
Breathing can remain in the middle of a larger, steadier field.

26
00:04:08.839 --> 00:04:16.019
On the next few exhales, see whether the jaw, hands, or shoulders release a small amount of unnecessary effort.

27
00:04:17.719 --> 00:04:19.219
Do not push them down or open.

28
00:04:20.919 --> 00:04:22.629
Keep the tension that feels useful.

29
00:04:24.329 --> 00:04:27.049
Let go only where the body offers a little room.

30
00:04:52.195 --> 00:04:54.985
Notice where stress is most obvious right now.

31
00:04:56.685 --> 00:05:01.825
Perhaps heat, pressure, movement, tightness, restlessness, or a crowded stream of thought.

32
00:05:03.525 --> 00:05:06.925
Name the sensation simply, without deciding what it means.

33
00:05:08.625 --> 00:05:10.455
Then return to one point of support.

34
00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:37.471
Move gently between them.

35
00:05:39.171 --> 00:05:40.881
One breath with the stressed place.

36
00:05:42.581 --> 00:05:45.171
One breath with the feet, seat, back, or hand.

37
00:05:46.871 --> 00:05:50.751
You are not avoiding the difficult sensation and you are not staring at it.

38
00:05:52.451 --> 00:05:55.081
You are practicing a wider attention that can hold both.

39
00:06:25.257 --> 00:06:29.497
If it feels comfortable, allow the exhale to finish without hurrying the next inhale.

40
00:06:31.197 --> 00:06:33.857
Do this once or twice, not as a rule.

41
00:06:35.557 --> 00:06:43.037
If any breath change brings strain, dizziness, or discomfort, return immediately to ordinary breathing and the support beneath you.

42
00:07:08.183 --> 00:07:11.353
Silently offer a plain phrase: this is a stressed moment.

43
00:07:13.053 --> 00:07:15.343
Or: I can meet one breath at a time.

44
00:07:17.043 --> 00:07:19.273
Use words only if they reduce effort.

45
00:07:20.973 --> 00:07:24.333
Otherwise, stay with sensation and sound.

46
00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:46.020
Let attention include the whole body.

47
00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:51.260
Notice that the body is larger than the most activated place.

48
00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:58.040
Feel its outline, its contact with clothing, and the air touching exposed skin.

49
00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:01.270
Stress may still be here.

50
00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:05.680
It does not have to occupy every part of awareness.

51
00:08:30.826 --> 00:08:33.696
Consider the next few minutes after this practice.

52
00:08:35.396 --> 00:08:44.246
Is there one small action that would make them more workable: drinking water, reducing one demand, stepping outside, asking for help, or writing down the next task?

53
00:08:45.946 --> 00:08:48.486
You do not need to solve the entire situation.

54
00:09:08.603 --> 00:09:11.343
Return to the natural breath for three quiet cycles.

55
00:09:13.043 --> 00:09:16.123
Notice each inhale arriving and each exhale leaving.

56
00:09:17.823 --> 00:09:19.573
Let the pauses be whatever they are.

57
00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:40.970
Orient outward again.

58
00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:44.970
Look toward light, color, and the edges of the room.

59
00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:47.950
Move the fingers and feet.

60
00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:53.640
Keep any steadiness that is available, without requiring yourself to feel calm.

61
00:09:56.040 --> 00:10:00.000
When you are ready, continue at a pace that respects the body you have now.
